{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791628909,"nonce":"oe8iyOg6/dikXjo9kJMwpryZVvI=","message":"Not a missing re-tier site. Traced on 3d5ab48 plus the new re-tier decision log (ASH_OSR_LOG=1 now says, per function, why no exit was made or where it was made), PoolBench with `--mode jit`:\n\n    [retier] findex=31 header=2: exit at pc=3 with 5 inputs        \u003c- job, the sqrt loop\n    [tier] install findex=31 tier=cranelift ... on ash-vm-3\n    [tiered] attempted=36 succeeded=5 failed=0 ... cranelift=34 llvm=0\n\n`job` has its exit and its Cranelift install. It is installed on `ash-vm-3`, a pool thread: a function first reached from compiled code on a worker goes through `resolve_worker_stub`, which compiles it through a worker bead (`worker_beads`) and installs it with `eager_install`. The request for the LLVM tier lives in `compiled_only_entry`, which only an interpreter entering the function runs, and it asks the adapter to `force_promote` a bead the adapter registered. A worker bead is neither, so nothing ever queues the tier-1 compile and `job` stays on Cranelift for the whole run. `main` (findex 192) is a different case: its loops are nested, so by design (retier_worth_polling) it has no exit.\n\nWhat a fix needs: after `resolve_worker_stub` installs a function that has a re-tier site, queue a tier-1 compile for it off the worker thread (the global worker_compile_lock is held across the install, so not there). That is a promotion queue for worker beads, not a change to the analysis, and it only matters for `--mode jit`; I am leaving it open.","files":null}]}